<section class="panel" aria-label="Grade 3 Unit 4 Lesson 8 Relate Quotients to Familiar Products"><h2>Source Number Talk</h2><p>4×10=40, 40÷4=10, 40÷10=4, 60÷6=10. Explain inverse multiplication/division.</p><h2>Personal multiplication card sort</h2><p>Work in pairs and sort each independent first-party multiplication fact into three personal categories: <strong>know it right away</strong>, <strong>can find it quickly</strong>, <strong>don't know it yet</strong>. Choose five personally difficult facts and practice with partner. Teacher checks work; none of the three categories has a universal correct grading result.</p><p>Independently authored practice grid (not publisher card-master artwork):</p><table class="source-chart"><thead><tr><th>First factor</th><th>Practice products with 1 through 9</th></tr></thead><tbody>@for (var a = 1; a <= 9; a++) { <tr><td>@a</td><td>@string.Join(" · ", Enumerable.Range(1,9).Select(b => $"{a}×{b}={a*b}"))</td></tr> }</tbody></table><h2>If I Know, Then I Know</h2><p>Original published example: 4×5=20 → 20÷4=5 and20÷5=4. For each student-selected card record multiplication plus both division facts.</p><p>Additional independently written examples:6×7=42 →42÷6=7 and42÷7=6;3×8=24 →24÷3=8 and24÷8=3.</p><p>Original blackline cards/photo and publisher-gated Cool-down not copied. <a href="https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-3/unit-4/lesson-8/lesson.html">2021 teacher source</a></p></section>
